The layout's cold set built in GHC.CmmToAsm: an experiment, not adopted
=======================================================================

A patch to GHC's native code generator, on top of the linear-allocator series:
the for-review branch of ~/slop/ghc at 4328c39640 ("Document behaviour
of the NCG's CFG, layout and register allocators", on merged-mrs 12bd4c0c7a).
Apply it from the root of a checkout of that commit:

    git apply ghc-ncg-layout-cold-set.patch

git apply skips this header.  The patch was checked with `git apply --check`
against that tree, and the compiler built from it.

What the series does
--------------------

The series treats the blocks that follow a failed heap or stack check as cold
in two places after register allocation: the chain layout's back-edge rule
(preferBackEdges in sequenceChain, GHC.CmmToAsm.BlockLayout) raises no back edge
leaving one, and static prediction (loopHeads in staticBranchPrediction,
GHC.CmmToAsm.CFG) counts no loop as headed by a block whose back edges all leave
one.  Both find the set with coldBlocks, which infers it from the final CFG:
checkFailures' blocks (those an edge tagged HeapStackCheck enters, and their
successors) and the blocks the register allocator put after one of them,
recognised by a single out-edge whose source is AsmCodeGen.

Two shapes escape that inference, as the TODO at coldBlocks says:

  - In a procedure needing more stack than maxSpillSlots, allocMoreStack splits
    each entry block into a stack allocation jumping to a new block that holds
    the old code.  Where the garbage collector returns to such a block, the new
    block carries the back edge, and its out-edges are the old block's,
    not the code generator's, so it is missed.

  - A fixup block F that the allocator puts on a check's failure edge H -> G
    takes that edge's HeapStackCheck tag on H -> F (addNodesBetween), so F
    counts as the failure target and G as its successor, and the block
    the garbage collector returns to, after G, is missed.

What the patch does
-------------------

It builds the set in GHC.CmmToAsm, where the blocks inserted after allocation
are known (layoutCold in cmmNativeGen): the allocator's set (checkFailures
of the native CFG, before any block is inserted), the block allocMoreStack moves
the code of a cold entry block into (stack_updt_blks), and the fixup blocks
on an edge out of or into a cold block (cfgRegAllocUpdates).  It passes the set
through optimizeCFG, staticPredCfg, mkGlobalWeights and staticBranchPrediction
to loopHeads, and through sequenceTop and sequenceChain to preferBackEdges,
in place of both calls of coldBlocks.  The graph allocator's call
of mkGlobalWeights (slurpSpillCostInfo) keeps coldBlocks, its frequencies
changing no code.

What it measured
----------------

On x86-64, against the series' tip without it:

  - nofib at -O2: the objects of real/fulsom, real/scs and spectral/hartel/ida
    change, each in one procedure laid out anew; their instruction counts move
    by +0.0000%, +0.0001% and +0.0008%;
  - containers' benchmarks: no object changes;
  - vector's benchmarks: none moves beyond its noise;
  - the compiler's allocation: perf/compiler's T9961 at -O0 +0.034%, T12707
    at -O0 +0.012%, T3294 at -O0 +0.005%, T9961 at -O +0.015%, the rest within
    the runs' spread; compiling nofib -0.008%.

The figures for nofib, containers and vector were taken on an earlier version
of the patch, whose code differed only in SpillCost's frequency map, since made
a where binding; the compiler's allocation, measured again with the patch
as it stands, came out the same to the byte.

Why it was not adopted
----------------------

It moved no figure measured, it costs compile allocation, and it threads a new
argument through calls that the open !16687 (per-procedure CPU target
attributes) also changes.  The TODO at coldBlocks records the experiment.

It serves only the series' uses of coldBlocks.  The other TODOs about
these blocks, at checkFailures and in the linear allocator (linearRA_SCCs'
deferral, wideMoveDests' candidates, and regClobbers, whose TODO measured
leaving them out), concern the set the allocator takes, before any block
that the patch's set adds exists, so that there the two sets are equal; what
they ask for is a better classification of that set (a continuation that a call
also returns to, a failure target that another edge also enters) or block
frequencies, as static prediction computed in a Cmm pass would give (Note
[Static Branch Prediction]).

